    "One‑Page Report\n",
    "\n",
    "Title: Natural Language → SQL from Minimal Schema Hints: Experiments & Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "Objective\n",
    "I evaluated whether a general LLM (GPT‑3.5‑Turbo) can pick relevant tables and generate correct SQL given only table names and short column definitions (no full DDL). The pipeline was: (1) convert a DataFrame schema into compact text; (2) prompt the model to select relevant tables as a JSON array; (3) prompt again to generate a single SQL query using only those tables; (4) execute against a toy SQLite database and log outcomes.\n",
    "\n",
    "Method\n",
    "I used four tables—students, courses, enrollments, departments—with minimal column descriptions. I designed six natural‑language questions covering filters, joins (2–3 tables), aggregation, grouping with thresholds, and an edge case deliberately referencing a column not in the schema (“GPA”) to test hallucination resistance. All prompts were constrained to (a) pure JSON on selection and (b) a single SQL code block on generation, with temperature=0 and explicit instructions to not invent columns or tables.\n",
    "\n",
    "Results\n",
    "\n",
    "Table selection: For straightforward questions (filters on age, course name matching, enrollment relationships), the model correctly returned [\"students\",\"enrollments\",\"courses\"]. For department joins, it added [\"departments\"].\n",
    "\n",
    "SQL generation: Queries were syntactically valid SQLite in most cases, using explicit JOINs and respecting listed columns. Aggregations like “count distinct students per course” were handled correctly (e.g., COUNT(DISTINCT e.student_id) with GROUP BY c.course_id, c.course_name).\n",
    "\n",
    "Execution: Most generated queries executed and returned expected rows on the toy DB. Minor formatting differences (aliases, capitalization) did not matter.\n",
    "\n",
    "Hallucination test (GPA): With the restrictive prompt, the model avoided inventing the gpa column in several runs. When it did falter, failure modes were: (i) referencing a non‑existent alias or column; (ii) proposing a plausible column like grade_point that wasn’t defined. Executing those queries raised SQL errors, which my notebook captures and logs.\n",
    "\n",
    "What did not work well / Variations\n",
    "\n",
    "If I omitted the “do not invent columns” rule, the model was more likely to hallucinate columns (e.g., gpa, credits_earned).\n",
    "\n",
    "If the schema text was verbose or inconsistent, selection sometimes included an unnecessary table (e.g., selected departments when not required).\n",
    "\n",
    "Returning clean JSON is fragile unless I demand “ONLY a pure JSON array (no code fences)”. The regex fallback helps, but strict instruction is best.\n",
    "\n",
    "Mitigations & Best Practices\n",
    "\n",
    "Schema discipline: Keep table descriptions short and consistent; list only needed columns.\n",
    "\n",
    "Guardrails: Explicitly say “Use ONLY these tables/columns” and “Do not invent schema.” Keep temperature=0.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two-stage prompting: Separate table selection and SQL generation. It increases reliability and debuggability.\n",
    "\n",
    "Validation loop: Execute the SQL automatically; if it fails, surface the error and optionally re‑prompt with the error message (“The column gpa does not exist; reconsider using only listed columns”).\n",
    "\n",
    "Evaluation: Track per‑question: selected tables, SQL, execution success, error text. This makes issues reproducible.\n",
    "\n",
    "Learnings\n",
    "\n",
    "LLMs can translate natural language into SQL with limited schema context, especially for standard joins and aggregations. However, they are sensitive to prompt clarity and schema noise, and they need strong constraints to avoid hallucinations. A pragmatic workflow is: (a) slim schema hints → (b) two‑step selection/generation → (c) execution validation → (d) corrective re‑prompting on failure. For production, add schema introspection (auto‑listing real columns) and enforce parsing/verification before running queries on live data."
   ]
